meta {
  name: 03 AI quota on speaking
  type: http
  seq: 4
}

post {
  url: {{baseUrl}}/api/speaking/evaluate
  body: json
  auth: none
}

headers {
  Authorization: Bearer {{studentAccessToken}}
}

body:json {
  {
    "teilNumber": 1,
    "transcript": "Ich heiße Awa und ich wohne in Douala. Ich lerne Deutsch, weil ich in Deutschland studieren möchte. Am Wochenende treffe ich meine Freunde und wir sprechen zusammen Deutsch."
  }
}

assert {
  res.status: eq 200
}

docs {
  The metered operation. Each SUCCESSFUL evaluation writes one `ai_usage` row,
  and the allowance is how many of those are newer than 24 hours ago.

  ## The allowances

  | tier | evaluations per rolling 24h |
  |---|---|
  | START | 2 |
  | PRO | 5 |
  | PREMIUM | 20 |
  | no tier (independent, or removed) | 2 |

  A trial is not a fourth number: it is Start with a fourteen-day clock and
  one seat.

  ## Spending an allowance

  With a START student, send this three times. The first two return 200; the
  third returns:

      {
        "error": "AI_QUOTA_EXCEEDED",
        "tier": "START",
        "usedToday": 2,
        "allowedToday": 2,
        "resetsAt": "2026-09-16T08:32:11.000Z"
      }

  It is a sales moment, not an error. Everything needed for "you have used 2
  of 2 today — back at 09:32, or ask your school about Pro" travels with the
  refusal.

  `resetsAt` is the OLDEST counted row plus 24 hours, not midnight. A rolling
  window has no midnight, and a fixed one would be wrong for everyone outside
  a single timezone — UTC midnight is 1am in Douala and 9am in Tokyo. Confirm
  it moves with the data:

      SELECT created_at FROM ai_usage WHERE student_id = '<student>'
       ORDER BY created_at LIMIT 1;

  ## A ROLLING window — the case worth proving

  Age one row out and the student comes straight back, with no job having run:

      UPDATE ai_usage SET created_at = now() - interval '25 hours'
       WHERE id = (SELECT id FROM ai_usage WHERE student_id = '<student>'
                    ORDER BY created_at LIMIT 1);

  Re-run: 200. There is no counter anywhere to reset, because the rows ARE the
  quota.

  ## A FAILED evaluation must cost nothing

  This is the ordering worth checking by hand, because it is the one a happy
  path never shows. Break the AI call — clear `GEMINI_API_KEY` in the
  environment the server is running with, or point it at a dead host — and
  send a request that would otherwise succeed.

  It fails, and `SELECT count(*) FROM ai_usage WHERE student_id = '<student>'`
  must NOT have grown. Metered APIs do not bill a 500: a Gemini failure would
  otherwise spend one of a Start student's two daily sessions and hand them
  nothing, and a bad afternoon on our side would cost them the day.

  The trade is that a genuine failure can be retried for free, so one counted
  session may cost us two calls to Gemini. That is the right way round — the
  cost of our failure lands on us.

  ## And a refused request must not reach Gemini

  With the allowance already spent, the check runs BEFORE the call. Watch the
  server log: a 403 AI_QUOTA_EXCEEDED must show no Gemini request at all.
  Checking afterwards would let a student over their limit still cost us the
  request, which is the expense the quota exists to bound.

  ## Independent students are metered too

  Release a student (`UPDATE students SET center_id = NULL ...`) and they get
  2, not unlimited. Leaving them unmetered would let a center release its
  whole roster to hand them free AI we pay for. A quota with a way around it
  is not a quota.

  ## Clearing usage between runs

      DELETE FROM ai_usage WHERE student_id = '<student>';
}
